<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Hafsid emirate of Bejaia</h1><p class="st-meta">1285 CE – 1309 CE (approx.) · capital Bejaia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A breakaway Hafsid realm based at Bejaia that split the dynasty in two for a generation before its rulers reunited Ifriqiya under their own line.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-i-al-mustansir">al-Mustansir</a> in 1277 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate</a> fell into succession disputes. In 1285 Abu Zakariya, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-ishaq-ibrahim-i">Abu Ishaq Ibrahim</a>, established himself at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bejaia">Bejaia</a> and soon held Constantine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, refusing to recognise his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-hafs-umar-i">Abu Hafs Umar</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>. The western provinces thus became a separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafsid-dynasty">Hafsid</a> state.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bejaia">Bejaia</a> was a prosperous port with strong ties to Aragon, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Genoa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, and a seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a> scholarship. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> governed the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> through the same Almohad-style institutions as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>, while facing pressure from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zayyanid-kingdom-of-tlemcen">Zayyanid Kingdom of Tlemcen</a> on their western border and from Arab tribes in the interior.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Abu Zakariya's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-baqa-khalid">Abu al-Baqa Khalid</a> succeeded in 1301 and, in 1309, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>, reuniting the two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafsid-dynasty">Hafsid</a> realms. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bejaia">Bejaia</a> and Constantine would again break away under rival princes later in the 14th century.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1285 CE – 1309 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:85.27%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1285 CE – 1309 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bejaia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abu Zakariya Yahya, Abu al-Baqa Khalid</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam (Maliki)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Berber</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hafsid Bejaia, Emirate of Bougie, Hafsid Constantine</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1297&amp;focus=hafsid-emirate-of-bejaia" aria-label="Hafsid emirate of Bejaia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4807 853 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hafsid emirate of Bejaia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5127 1109L5255 1107"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5255 1107h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5127 1109h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5127 1109h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bejaia. <a href="/atlas?year=1297&amp;focus=hafsid-emirate-of-bejaia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
